1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,470
A wave of molasses,

2
00:00:01,470 --> 00:00:02,830
twenty-five feet high,

3
00:00:02,830 --> 00:00:05,070
racing through a city at thirty-five miles

4
00:00:05,070 --> 00:00:07,382
an hour. It sounds like a joke.

5
00:00:07,382 --> 00:00:09,424
But it killed twenty-one people.

6
00:00:09,424 --> 00:00:10,824
So how does a tank of sweet

7
00:00:10,824 --> 00:00:12,278
syrup turn into a killer?

8
00:00:16,067 --> 00:00:17,147
January 15th,

9
00:00:17,147 --> 00:00:18,529
1919

10
00:00:18,529 --> 00:00:21,129
Boston is enjoying a strangely warm winter day

11
00:00:21,731 --> 00:00:21,731
In the crowded North End,

12
00:00:23,111 --> 00:00:24,711
kids are playing in the street,

13
00:00:24,711 --> 00:00:26,151
workers are eating lunch,

14
00:00:26,151 --> 00:00:28,751
and horses pull wagons along the waterfront

15
00:00:28,954 --> 00:00:29,004
Then,

16
00:00:29,304 --> 00:00:29,884
out of nowhere,

17
00:00:29,884 --> 00:00:31,179
a deep rumble

18
00:00:31,179 --> 00:00:33,593
And a rattle like machine-gun fire

19
00:00:33,593 --> 00:00:33,593
A giant steel tank rips open...

20
00:00:36,058 --> 00:00:37,498
and a wave of molasses,

21
00:00:37,498 --> 00:00:38,578
taller than a house,

22
00:00:38,578 --> 00:00:40,393
comes roaring down the street

23
00:00:40,393 --> 00:00:40,393
Sounds like a joke,

24
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:42,345
right?

25
00:00:42,345 --> 00:00:43,480
It wasn't

26
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,120
It became one of the deadliest,

27
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:46,040
and strangest,

28
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:48,120
disasters in American history

29
00:00:48,222 --> 00:00:48,222
And when it was over,

30
00:00:49,282 --> 00:00:52,238
the company behind it would point the finger...

31
00:00:52,238 --> 00:00:53,845
at someone else

32
00:00:53,595 --> 00:00:53,845
This is the true story of the Great Molasses

33
00:00:56,185 --> 00:00:57,305
Flood

34
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:02,080
To understand what happened,

35
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:04,120
we have to go back to 1915,

36
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:06,614
when a giant steel tank went up on Boston's

37
00:01:06,614 --> 00:01:07,734
waterfront

38
00:01:07,568 --> 00:01:07,838
It belonged to a company called United States

39
00:01:10,617 --> 00:01:11,857
Industrial Alcohol,

40
00:01:11,857 --> 00:01:13,577
and it stood on Commercial Street,

41
00:01:13,577 --> 00:01:15,436
right in the North End

42
00:01:15,166 --> 00:01:15,436
And it was enormous

43
00:01:17,019 --> 00:01:17,899
Fifty feet tall,

44
00:01:17,899 --> 00:01:19,019
ninety feet wide,

45
00:01:19,019 --> 00:01:21,139
holding more than two million gallons of

46
00:01:21,139 --> 00:01:22,259
molasses

47
00:01:22,143 --> 00:01:22,413
That's enough to fill more than three Olympic

48
00:01:24,593 --> 00:01:26,014
swimming pools

49
00:01:25,744 --> 00:01:26,014
But why would anyone need that much molasses?

50
00:01:28,892 --> 00:01:29,572
It's the thick,

51
00:01:29,572 --> 00:01:32,052
dark syrup left over when sugar is made from

52
00:01:32,052 --> 00:01:33,433
sugar cane

53
00:01:33,163 --> 00:01:33,433
And it could be turned into alcohol

54
00:01:35,538 --> 00:01:37,376
Alcohol for drinks...

55
00:01:37,376 --> 00:01:39,736
and for making explosives in the First World

56
00:01:39,736 --> 00:01:40,716
War

57
00:01:40,446 --> 00:01:40,716
With a war raging,

58
00:01:41,656 --> 00:01:43,150
business was booming

59
00:01:43,150 --> 00:01:44,350
The company wanted more,

60
00:01:44,350 --> 00:01:44,910
and more,

61
00:01:44,910 --> 00:01:46,313
and more

62
00:01:46,043 --> 00:01:46,313
Ship after ship sailed in from the Caribbean,

63
00:01:48,773 --> 00:01:51,103
filling the tank again and again

64
00:01:50,833 --> 00:01:51,103
And right next to it?

65
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:53,780
Thousands of people,

66
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:55,740
in one of the busiest neighborhoods in the

67
00:01:55,740 --> 00:01:56,860
city

68
00:01:56,442 --> 00:01:56,862
Many were Italian immigrant families,

69
00:01:59,162 --> 00:02:00,762
packed into small apartments,

70
00:02:00,762 --> 00:02:02,762
just a few streets from a giant tank of

71
00:02:02,762 --> 00:02:03,882
syrup

72
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:09,753
Here's where things start to go wrong

73
00:02:09,753 --> 00:02:11,273
The tank was built in a hurry,

74
00:02:11,273 --> 00:02:13,873
and the man in charge wasn't even an engineer

75
00:02:14,202 --> 00:02:14,472
He never asked an engineer,

76
00:02:16,092 --> 00:02:17,012
or an architect,

77
00:02:17,012 --> 00:02:18,372
to check the plans

78
00:02:18,372 --> 00:02:19,812
Not once

79
00:02:19,569 --> 00:02:19,839
Was it tested?

80
00:02:21,453 --> 00:02:22,433
Barely

81
00:02:22,433 --> 00:02:24,633
They poured in just a few inches of water,

82
00:02:24,633 --> 00:02:26,367
and called it done

83
00:02:26,097 --> 00:02:26,367
A real test meant filling it with water all

84
00:02:28,707 --> 00:02:29,667
the way to the top,

85
00:02:29,667 --> 00:02:32,010
to see if it could take the weight

86
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:32,010
And almost right away,

87
00:02:33,230 --> 00:02:34,936
the tank began to leak

88
00:02:34,936 --> 00:02:37,441
Brown streaks ran down its sides

89
00:02:37,621 --> 00:02:40,261
Local kids even collected the dripping molasses

90
00:02:40,261 --> 00:02:42,153
as a free sweet treat

91
00:02:41,883 --> 00:02:42,153
One worker who looked after the tank warned his

92
00:02:44,733 --> 00:02:46,410
boss about the leaks...

93
00:02:46,410 --> 00:02:48,810
and about strange cracking sounds

94
00:02:48,576 --> 00:02:48,846
The answer he got?

95
00:02:50,642 --> 00:02:52,398
Nothing was wrong

96
00:02:52,128 --> 00:02:52,398
So what did the company do about the leaks?

97
00:02:55,261 --> 00:02:56,661
It painted the tank brown,

98
00:02:56,661 --> 00:02:59,261
so the leaks would be harder to see

99
00:02:58,852 --> 00:02:59,272
The leaks were hidden

100
00:03:00,841 --> 00:03:02,921
The danger was not

101
00:03:06,633 --> 00:03:08,570
January 1919

102
00:03:08,570 --> 00:03:11,010
A ship arrives in Boston with a fresh load

103
00:03:11,010 --> 00:03:12,109
of molasses

104
00:03:12,109 --> 00:03:14,215
It will be the last delivery

105
00:03:13,945 --> 00:03:14,215
Warm molasses from the ship is pumped in,

106
00:03:16,595 --> 00:03:19,195
right on top of the cold syrup already inside

107
00:03:19,649 --> 00:03:19,919
The tank is filled almost to the brim

108
00:03:22,202 --> 00:03:24,282
About 2.3 million gallons

109
00:03:24,530 --> 00:03:24,800
How heavy is that?

110
00:03:26,505 --> 00:03:28,105
More than twelve thousand tons,

111
00:03:28,105 --> 00:03:30,705
all pushing outward on thin steel walls

112
00:03:30,855 --> 00:03:31,125
For days,

113
00:03:31,745 --> 00:03:33,472
it's been freezing cold

114
00:03:33,472 --> 00:03:35,681
Then the weather does something strange

115
00:03:35,681 --> 00:03:37,620
It suddenly warms up

116
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:37,620
On January 14th,

117
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,149
it's around two degrees Fahrenheit

118
00:03:41,149 --> 00:03:42,547
The next day?

119
00:03:42,547 --> 00:03:43,987
Over forty

120
00:03:43,815 --> 00:03:44,085
Some historians think the molasses may have

121
00:03:46,505 --> 00:03:47,505
started to ferment,

122
00:03:47,505 --> 00:03:50,105
making gas that pushed against the walls

123
00:03:50,108 --> 00:03:50,378
Inside the tank,

124
00:03:51,438 --> 00:03:53,435
the pressure keeps building

125
00:03:53,165 --> 00:03:53,435
Neighbors said that when it was full,

126
00:03:55,175 --> 00:03:56,975
the tank groaned and rumbled,

127
00:03:56,975 --> 00:03:59,267
almost as if it were alive

128
00:03:58,847 --> 00:03:59,267
And on the morning of January 15th,

129
00:04:01,527 --> 00:04:04,127
nobody imagined what was about to happen

130
00:04:08,433 --> 00:04:09,740
It's lunchtime

131
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:11,020
City workers are eating,

132
00:04:11,020 --> 00:04:13,572
and kids are walking home from school

133
00:04:13,302 --> 00:04:13,572
At about half past twelve...

134
00:04:15,710 --> 00:04:17,755
the tank splits apart

135
00:04:17,534 --> 00:04:17,755
Witnesses hear a sound like thunder,

136
00:04:19,694 --> 00:04:22,294
and the ground shakes beneath their feet

137
00:04:22,041 --> 00:04:22,311
Steel rivets shoot out like bullets

138
00:04:24,547 --> 00:04:27,079
And a wall of molasses bursts free

139
00:04:26,859 --> 00:04:27,079
How big was the wave?

140
00:04:29,003 --> 00:04:30,523
Up to twenty-five feet high,

141
00:04:30,523 --> 00:04:33,318
moving at around thirty-five miles per hour

142
00:04:33,318 --> 00:04:35,633
Faster than anyone could run

143
00:04:35,413 --> 00:04:35,633
Within seconds,

144
00:04:36,393 --> 00:04:38,773
the streets are buried under molasses two to

145
00:04:38,773 --> 00:04:40,301
three feet deep

146
00:04:40,031 --> 00:04:40,301
It crushes buildings,

147
00:04:41,481 --> 00:04:43,681
bends the steel of the elevated railway,

148
00:04:43,681 --> 00:04:46,281
and sweeps away wagons and horses

149
00:04:46,198 --> 00:04:46,418
A fire station is knocked right off its

150
00:04:48,598 --> 00:04:49,745
foundation...

151
00:04:49,745 --> 00:04:51,825
with firefighters still inside

152
00:04:51,829 --> 00:04:52,099
A house is torn apart

153
00:04:53,887 --> 00:04:56,131
A city building is swept away

154
00:04:55,861 --> 00:04:56,131
And for the people in its path...

155
00:04:58,310 --> 00:05:00,910
there was no time to run

156
00:05:04,233 --> 00:05:05,157
It's just syrup,

157
00:05:05,157 --> 00:05:05,939
right?

158
00:05:05,939 --> 00:05:07,606
How bad can it be?

159
00:05:07,606 --> 00:05:09,166
But once molasses catches you,

160
00:05:09,166 --> 00:05:11,015
it doesn't let go

161
00:05:10,745 --> 00:05:11,015
It's almost one and a half times as heavy

162
00:05:13,275 --> 00:05:14,466
as water

163
00:05:14,196 --> 00:05:14,466
And the more you struggle,

164
00:05:15,766 --> 00:05:17,687
the tighter it grips

165
00:05:17,417 --> 00:05:17,687
Even strong horses couldn't pull themselves

166
00:05:19,947 --> 00:05:20,898
free

167
00:05:20,628 --> 00:05:20,898
Then came the cold

168
00:05:22,539 --> 00:05:23,739
As the afternoon chilled,

169
00:05:23,739 --> 00:05:26,139
the molasses grew even thicker

170
00:05:25,905 --> 00:05:26,175
Everything was brown

171
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:29,600
You couldn't tell where the street ended and

172
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:31,291
the syrup began

173
00:05:31,061 --> 00:05:31,291
Rescuers waded in up to their waists,

174
00:05:33,471 --> 00:05:35,673
trying to pull people out

175
00:05:35,403 --> 00:05:35,673
Some rescuers got stuck too,

176
00:05:37,453 --> 00:05:39,903
and had to be pulled out themselves

177
00:05:39,633 --> 00:05:39,903
In the end,

178
00:05:40,523 --> 00:05:42,403
twenty-one people lost their lives,

179
00:05:42,403 --> 00:05:45,003
and around one hundred and fifty were injured

180
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:45,870
Among them were children,

181
00:05:47,250 --> 00:05:48,210
city workers,

182
00:05:48,210 --> 00:05:49,970
and a firefighter

183
00:05:53,667 --> 00:05:55,352
So who came to help?

184
00:05:55,352 --> 00:05:57,352
Some of the very first were sailors from a

185
00:05:57,352 --> 00:05:59,093
ship docked nearby

186
00:05:58,823 --> 00:05:59,093
They were young cadets from a training ship,

187
00:06:01,033 --> 00:06:03,113
and more than a hundred of them ran straight

188
00:06:03,113 --> 00:06:04,493
into the mess

189
00:06:04,223 --> 00:06:04,493
Police,

190
00:06:05,293 --> 00:06:05,993
firefighters,

191
00:06:05,993 --> 00:06:08,593
doctors and nurses rushed in right behind them

192
00:06:08,993 --> 00:06:09,263
With ropes,

193
00:06:10,003 --> 00:06:11,563
ladders and their bare hands,

194
00:06:11,563 --> 00:06:13,782
they searched through the wreckage

195
00:06:13,512 --> 00:06:13,782
The injured were carried to a nearby hospital,

196
00:06:16,242 --> 00:06:18,842
many covered head to toe in syrup

197
00:06:18,573 --> 00:06:18,843
The rescue went on through the night,

198
00:06:20,543 --> 00:06:22,454
by the light of lamps

199
00:06:22,184 --> 00:06:22,454
At the fire station,

200
00:06:23,814 --> 00:06:25,874
firefighters worked for hours to free their

201
00:06:25,874 --> 00:06:27,314
trapped friends

202
00:06:27,121 --> 00:06:27,391
The search for the missing went on for days

203
00:06:29,965 --> 00:06:30,235
And some of the missing weren't found for

204
00:06:32,015 --> 00:06:33,053
months

205
00:06:32,783 --> 00:06:33,053
It was one of the darkest days the neighborhood

206
00:06:35,313 --> 00:06:37,073
had ever seen

207
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:42,660
And then came a problem no city had ever

208
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:43,357
faced

209
00:06:43,357 --> 00:06:45,945
How do you clean up a flood of molasses?

210
00:06:45,675 --> 00:06:45,945
Hundreds of workers came to scrub the streets

211
00:06:48,816 --> 00:06:49,086
They sprayed salt water from the harbor to wash

212
00:06:51,466 --> 00:06:53,223
the molasses away

213
00:06:52,953 --> 00:06:53,223
Why salt water?

214
00:06:54,889 --> 00:06:56,706
Fresh water barely worked

215
00:06:56,706 --> 00:06:59,106
Salt water cut through the sticky syrup much

216
00:06:59,106 --> 00:07:00,042
better

217
00:06:59,772 --> 00:07:00,042
Sand was spread on the streets,

218
00:07:01,622 --> 00:07:04,207
so people could walk without sticking

219
00:07:03,937 --> 00:07:04,207
Still,

220
00:07:04,707 --> 00:07:06,159
it got everywhere

221
00:07:06,159 --> 00:07:07,959
People tracked it all over Boston,

222
00:07:07,959 --> 00:07:08,879
on their shoes,

223
00:07:08,879 --> 00:07:10,959
into streetcars and homes

224
00:07:10,837 --> 00:07:11,107
Door handles,

225
00:07:12,127 --> 00:07:12,767
benches,

226
00:07:12,767 --> 00:07:15,367
even telephones across the city turned sticky

227
00:07:15,579 --> 00:07:15,849
The water in the harbor stayed brown for

228
00:07:17,989 --> 00:07:19,000
months

229
00:07:18,730 --> 00:07:19,000
In total,

230
00:07:19,660 --> 00:07:21,580
the cleanup took more than eighty-seven

231
00:07:21,580 --> 00:07:23,479
thousand hours of work

232
00:07:23,209 --> 00:07:23,479
And for decades,

233
00:07:24,499 --> 00:07:26,459
people swore the neighborhood still smelled of

234
00:07:26,459 --> 00:07:28,671
molasses on hot summer days

235
00:07:28,671 --> 00:07:30,710
But the strangest part of this story was still

236
00:07:30,710 --> 00:07:32,150
to come

237
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:37,730
So who was responsible?

238
00:07:37,730 --> 00:07:40,210
The families of the victims took the company to

239
00:07:40,210 --> 00:07:41,189
court

240
00:07:40,919 --> 00:07:41,189
More than a hundred lawsuits were filed,

241
00:07:43,249 --> 00:07:45,849
and the courts joined them into one giant case

242
00:07:46,085 --> 00:07:46,355
But the company had a surprising answer

243
00:07:48,901 --> 00:07:49,171
It said the tank hadn't failed at all

244
00:07:51,432 --> 00:07:53,272
It had been blown up by anarchists,

245
00:07:53,272 --> 00:07:54,730
with a bomb

246
00:07:54,910 --> 00:07:56,990
Anarchists were radicals who wanted to bring

247
00:07:56,990 --> 00:07:59,390
down governments and big companies

248
00:07:59,257 --> 00:07:59,527
And there had been real bombings in America at

249
00:08:01,987 --> 00:08:02,507
the time,

250
00:08:02,507 --> 00:08:04,803
so some people believed it

251
00:08:04,533 --> 00:08:04,803
Here's the catch:

252
00:08:05,583 --> 00:08:06,543
if it was a bomb,

253
00:08:06,543 --> 00:08:09,143
the company wouldn't have to pay a cent

254
00:08:08,886 --> 00:08:09,156
Its lawyers spent years trying to prove it

255
00:08:11,604 --> 00:08:11,873
It was a clever way to dodge the blame

256
00:08:14,387 --> 00:08:14,857
But the families,

257
00:08:15,836 --> 00:08:16,836
and their lawyers,

258
00:08:16,836 --> 00:08:18,916
refused to give up

259
00:08:22,533 --> 00:08:24,413
What followed became one of the biggest

260
00:08:24,413 --> 00:08:26,493
lawsuits in Massachusetts history

261
00:08:26,783 --> 00:08:27,053
The court chose a special auditor to hear all

262
00:08:29,513 --> 00:08:30,837
the evidence

263
00:08:30,567 --> 00:08:30,837
It dragged on for years,

264
00:08:32,217 --> 00:08:34,977
with hundreds of witnesses and experts on both

265
00:08:34,977 --> 00:08:35,993
sides

266
00:08:35,723 --> 00:08:35,993
Witnesses talked about the leaks,

267
00:08:38,013 --> 00:08:39,365
the groaning...

268
00:08:39,365 --> 00:08:41,124
and the brown paint

269
00:08:40,854 --> 00:08:41,124
Engineers studied the broken steel,

270
00:08:43,064 --> 00:08:44,745
and did the math

271
00:08:44,475 --> 00:08:44,745
And the math was clear:

272
00:08:45,925 --> 00:08:48,525
the steel walls were simply too thin to hold

273
00:08:48,525 --> 00:08:49,925
that much weight

274
00:08:49,655 --> 00:08:49,925
The company's experts still insisted it was a

275
00:08:52,345 --> 00:08:53,321
bomb

276
00:08:53,051 --> 00:08:53,291
Then,

277
00:08:53,661 --> 00:08:55,301
in 1925,

278
00:08:55,301 --> 00:08:56,473
the verdict

279
00:08:56,473 --> 00:08:57,850
There was no bomb

280
00:08:57,850 --> 00:08:59,090
The tank was too weak,

281
00:08:59,090 --> 00:09:01,690
and it had never been properly tested

282
00:09:01,516 --> 00:09:01,786
The company had to pay the victims about six

283
00:09:04,286 --> 00:09:06,686
hundred and twenty-eight thousand dollars

284
00:09:06,718 --> 00:09:07,138
That's worth many millions of dollars today

285
00:09:13,967 --> 00:09:16,237
But this story didn't end in a courtroom

286
00:09:16,237 --> 00:09:18,837
It changed the way big things get built

287
00:09:18,634 --> 00:09:18,904
Before the flood,

288
00:09:19,664 --> 00:09:22,164
a company could build a giant tank with almost

289
00:09:22,164 --> 00:09:24,313
no expert checks at all

290
00:09:24,043 --> 00:09:24,313
Afterward,

291
00:09:24,973 --> 00:09:27,733
Massachusetts and other states began requiring

292
